# Task Management Guidelines

## Task Breakdown Process
- Analyze complex requests and break them down into discrete, manageable tasks.
- Organize tasks hierarchically with main tasks and subtasks.
- Prioritize tasks based on dependencies and learning progression.
- Use a consistent format for task definitions that includes:
  - Task ID
  - Task description
  - Expected outcome
  - Estimated complexity (Low/Medium/High)
  - Dependencies (if any)

## Task Tracking
- Maintain an updated task list across conversation sessions.
- Reference the current task being addressed at the beginning of each response.
- Mark tasks as completed once finished and summarize outcomes.
- Revisit incomplete tasks and explain how they connect to the current work.
- Periodically provide a task status overview to maintain learning momentum.

## Task Template

```
TASK [ID]: [Brief title]
Description: [Detailed description of what needs to be done]
Expected outcome: [What success looks like for this task]
Complexity: [Low/Medium/High]
Dependencies: [List of task IDs this task depends on, if any]
Status: [Not Started/In Progress/Completed]
```

## Progressive Learning Path
- Start with foundational tasks before moving to advanced concepts.
- Include small wins early to build confidence.
- Create learning checkpoints at the end of significant tasks to verify understanding.
- Provide reflection questions after completing major tasks to reinforce learning.
- Use a spiral approach to revisit key concepts with increasing depth.